<body>
<h1>Unit 15: Refraction at Plane Surfaces</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Waves &amp; Optics</p><p class="summary">Refraction at a plane boundary is Snell's law, but the interesting physics is in the consequences: the lateral shift through a slab, the shallower apparent depth of a pool, and total internal reflection, which is the whole basis of fibre optics.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Snell's law reads n1 sin(theta1) = n2 sin(theta2), with n = c/v the refractive index; the frequency of light is unchanged at the boundary while its speed and wavelength change.</li><li>A plane-parallel slab displaces the emergent ray sideways while keeping it parallel to the incident ray; the displacement grows with thickness and with obliquity.</li><li>Total internal reflection requires n1 &gt; n2 and an incidence angle above the critical angle theta_c = arcsin(n2/n1), about 41.1 degrees for crown glass (n = 1.52) against air.</li><li>Viewed near-normally, an object in water appears shallower by a factor of about n, so a 1.5 m depth reads as about 1.1 m.</li><li>Optical fibres work by repeated total internal reflection; the acceptance angle is fixed by the numerical aperture of the core and cladding.</li><li>In the wave picture refraction follows from phase continuity at the boundary: the phase must match across it, which is Snell's law in a different costume.</li><li>Snell's law is a statement about phase continuity across a boundary: the change of direction is what keeps the wavefronts in step, and the same law governs light as a wave and as a quantum field.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Snell's law assumes an isotropic, homogeneous, stationary, non-absorbing medium and a smooth interface; rough or moving boundaries change the result.</li><li>Near the critical angle the transmitted ray grazes the surface and the reflection coefficient approaches one.</li><li>The simple apparent-depth rule (divide by n) holds only for near-normal viewing; at oblique angles the correct factor is cos(theta')/cos(theta).</li><li>Dispersion - the wavelength dependence of n - is neglected in the basic model but must be included whenever white light is involved.</li><li>Fermat's principle fixes the path of least time rather than least distance, and that distinction is why a refracted ray bends towards the normal in a denser medium.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 4 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Laws of refraction: refractive index</li><li>Relation between refractive indices</li><li>Lateral shift</li><li>Total internal reflection</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Light slows down in glass and keeps that slower speed when it comes back into air, so the emerging ray is displaced but otherwise unchanged.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Speed is a property of the wave in the medium, and the medium it is in determines it. On returning to air the speed returns to c; what survives is the direction set by Snell's law.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> The speed is c/n in the medium, so it returns to c on leaving the glass. Snell's law n1 sin(theta1) = n2 sin(theta2) is satisfied at each boundary, and because the two faces of a parallel slab are parallel the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray, displaced sideways by t sin(i - r)/cos r.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Total internal reflection happens when the angle of incidence is large enough, whatever the two media.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> It requires the ray to travel from the optically denser medium to the optically rarer one. Going the other way (air into glass) the ray always emerges, because the refracted angle can never exceed 90 degrees.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> TIR needs n1 &gt; n2 and theta1 greater than the critical angle, sin(theta_c) = n2/n1. For glass to air the critical angle is about 41.8 degrees (n = 1.5), and for water to air about 48.6 degrees (n = 1.333).</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A coin at the bottom of a bowl of water appears at its true depth, because light travels in straight lines.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Light travels in straight lines in each medium, but the refraction at the surface bends the ray towards the normal, so the eye traces the ray back in a straight line and gets the wrong origin.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> For near-normal viewing the apparent depth is h' = h (n2/n1) = h / n for an object in water viewed from air. A 10 cm deep pool therefore looks about 7.5 cm deep with n = 1.333.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A ray of light in water (n = 1.333) meets the water-air surface at an angle of incidence of 40 degrees. Find the angle of refraction in air, and state whether total internal reflection would occur at this incidence.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Snell's law: 1.333 sin 40 degrees = 1 x sin(theta2), so sin(theta2) = 1.333 x 0.6428 = 0.8569 and theta2 = 58.9 degrees (about 59 degrees). The critical angle for water to air satisfies sin(theta_c) = 1/1.333 = 0.7502, so theta_c = 48.6 degrees. Since 40 degrees &lt; 48.6 degrees, the ray is well below the critical angle and no total internal reflection occurs.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive the expression for the lateral shift d produced when a ray passes through a plane-parallel slab of thickness t and refractive index n, with angle of incidence i and angle of refraction r.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Inside the slab the ray travels along a path inclined at r to the normal, so over the thickness t it moves laterally by t tan r. If the emergent ray were continued straight it would travel from the point of incidence at angle i and would be displaced by t tan i. The lateral shift is the difference between these two offsets measured at the exit plane. The perpendicular distance between the incident and emergent rays is d cos r, and the geometry gives d cos r = t tan i - t tan r, so d = t (tan i - tan r) / cos r. Using Snell's law, sin i = n sin r, this can be written d = t sin(i - r) / cos r, which vanishes when i = r (normal incidence).</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>Explain why an optical fibre guides light, and deduce the condition on the angle of entry for a glass core of index 1.5 surrounded by cladding of index 1.4.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Light entering the core strikes the core-cladding boundary from inside the optically denser medium. If the angle of incidence at that boundary exceeds the critical angle, sin(theta_c) = n_clad/n_core = 1.4/1.5 = 0.9333, so theta_c = 68.9 degrees, the ray is totally internally reflected and is guided along the fibre with no energy lost out of the core. In practice the light bounces many times per millimetre, so even a small loss per bounce is negligible. The acceptance condition in terms of the angle theta of the entering ray to the fibre axis is sin(theta) &lt;= sqrt(n_core^2 - n_clad^2) = sqrt(2.25 - 1.96) = sqrt(0.29) = 0.539, so theta &lt;= 32.6 degrees; this number is the numerical aperture of the fibre, and it sets how much light can be coupled in.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Refractive index of crown glass</td><td>about 1.52</td></tr><tr><td>Refractive index of water</td><td>about 1.333</td></tr><tr><td>Critical angle, crown glass to air</td><td>about 41.1 degrees</td></tr><tr><td>Critical angle for water to air</td><td>about 48.6 degrees</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can an image be formed that is not limited by the diffraction limit?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Superlenses using negative-index materials beat the diffraction limit at a single frequency; extending the idea to a usable bandwidth remains an open and difficult problem.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why does light slow down in a medium without losing energy?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>The classical account through the material's dielectric response is standard, while the fully microscopic picture of coherent forward scattering is subtler.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Compute the lateral shift of a ray through a glass slab and verify the prediction with a pencil, a glass block and a sheet of paper.</li><li>Design an optical fibre for a given numerical aperture and explain the role of the cladding.</li><li>Explain why a swimming pool looks shallower at the far end, and derive the apparent depth for oblique viewing.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/refraction-at-plane-surfaces">Read the chapter notes: Refraction at Plane Surfaces</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-refraction">Refraction 3D lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-optics">Optics theory lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 3</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/1-3-refraction">OpenStax: Refraction</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/1-4-total-internal-reflection">OpenStax: Total Internal Reflection</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
